well your excellency it's a great  pleasure to be here thank you very much  for the warm hospitable welcome uh and  it was a  a an important opportunity um and a  great honor to meet with uh his highness  the amir as well as his excellency  sheikh muhammad we discussed  as well as afghanistan the bilateral  relationship which we cherish and we  prize and we're excited about building  on in the weeks and months ahead but  obviously afghanistan has been at the  forefront of what we discussed i had the  opportunity to visit the compound  which is accommodating afghan refugees  here in doha and hear of the  heart-rending stories  it reminded me of my father who came to  the uk age six  and we will extend  a warm welcome and provide haven to  those  who  wish to come to the uk  and who are eligible our commitment uh  on the part of the uk to  afghanistan remains  we need to adjust to the new reality and  i think we had good conversations about  what that will involve  um it's worth saying that in the  airlift between the 15th of august and  the end of the month  we secured the safe passage of over 15  000 people out of afghanistan um the  biggest operation of its kind in in our  living memory and in no small part  because of the cooperation from our  qatari friends so i want to really  emphasize how much we appreciate that  i think in challenging times you really  know  the meaning of friendship and certainly  qatar has done an exceptional job  now of course we need to adjust to the  new reality and our immediate priority  is to secure the safe passage of those  remaining british nationals but also the  afghans who worked for the united  kingdom  and indeed others who may be at most  risk  again we're working closely on that and  uh  looking at the practicalities of of how  that can work  the uk center rapid deployment team to  this region uh because we want to make  sure working with third countries that  we can process both the nationals but  also the afghan workers under the arab  scheme and anyone else um and support  third countries uh in their efforts  we're also very mindful of the the  regional challenge that uh that  a flow of afghans from afghanistan will  create the instability at risk creating  for those neighbors  one of the reasons  we're doubling our aid spending to 286  million pounds is to try and help  support those countries bearing the  brunt  i want to thank qatar for the incredible  work they're doing  to support us in almost every effort  including hosting the united kingdom's  relocated embassy to afghanistan here  we do want to make sure we can  retain lines of engagement because  that's how we will adjust to the new  reality that i mentioned  it is uh going to be a challenge and it  will require a new set of strategies  so i think there are four key things  that we've discussed and we are very  like-minded on the first is making sure  afghanistan is not a haven or harbor to  terrorists again in the future  secondly preventing a humanitarian  crisis  and again that's why we've doubled our  aid spending for afghanistan  this year the knock-on effect of that is  third to present and to preserve  regional stability and fourth  to test and hold to account the taliban  on their commitments uh to progress to a  more inclusive government and obviously  we'll be looking very carefully at what  happens in the field of human rights and  the treatment of women and girls  i think it will be important to build an  international coalition around this i  think our qatari friends are clearly  a linchpin influential player  in that um  and  we are working with our g7 presidency  our nato membership uh we obviously  secured with the french the germans and  the other members of the security  council a resolution um recent days in  new york which i think is important it  provided i think a good framework that  all countries should be able to agree on  as a way for moving forward  now we need to get why they're buying  regional countries  involved  clearly there's a lot of countries with  a direct stake in what happens in  afghanistan as well as countries  who will feel and be moved by the  humanitarian risks and the plight  i think above all we need to put a  grouping together that can exert maximum  moderating influence on what the taliban  does next and we will certainly be  judging them  yes on their words but more importantly  on what they are willing to do to live  up to the assurances that they've made  we'll certainly keep working with all of  those partners  none more energetically than our qatari  friends to to make sure that we can  preserve the gains that have been made  in afghanistan over the last 20 years  and continue to support the afghan  people  thank you thank you  question for the qatari foreign minister  you've been intimately involved in the  important practical but also symbolic  regeneration of the airport could you  give us an update  on when you think it might open  and for mr raab what sense of guilt do  you feel over the people who have  supported britain both britain's  themselves and people who supported  britain that you've left behind  [Music]  some regarding  the airport actually we are still  in the evaluation process  there is no  clear indication when it's going to be  fully operational yet but we are working  very hard and also  engaging with baliban to identify what  are  the gaps and the risks  for  having the airport back up and running  but  we will remain hopeful that we will be  able  to operate it as soon as possible as  we  mentioned earlier  it's a very important and very strong  statement also  if taliban will demonstrate  their commitments to provide safe  passage  and freedom of movement for the people  of afghanistan so  we are trying our best to help  in serving uh that interest and  we will do our best we are engaging with  them engaging with also  turkey if they can provide any technical  assistance on on that front  and hopefully in the next few days we  will hear some good news  and on the question for me look  we've secured safe passage out of  afghanistan for over 17 000 british  nationals afghan workers  other special cases since april but i do  think we feel a responsibility to make  sure that the remaining british  nationals and afghan workers uh can come  to the uk that's why uh we watch with  great interest what may be possible at  cabil airport but we're also here and  i'm here not just in qatar but moving on  afterwards to talk to regional countries  about how we can  ensure safe passage through third  countries  the next question from the bbc  my first question is that you said mr  rob that there are new realities so do  you see any working relationship with  the taliban and keeping in mind the new  realities on the ground and secondly we  have been getting reports that  dozens of contractors who  used to work with the british embassy in  kabul they have been left behind could  you please tell us why and what is your  message to them because they are hiding  in other people who used to work with  the uk funded organizations thanks a lot  thank you look we are  pragmatic and realistic  we don't recognize  uh governments we will not be  recognizing uh the taliban but we do see  the need to be able to have direct  engagement otherwise we can't  provide messages we can't listen to the  response  and i think  as my friend sheikh mohammed said i  think there are some early tests for the  taliban one was their approach to the  airport during the evacuation now we're  into a different space  can we see safe passage provided  out of afghanistan that would be quite  an important first test we need to be  able to discuss that or engage on that  and there'll be other issues um  we all want to avoid a humanitarian  disaster or crisis  well that will require the taliban to be  able to  provide and ensure a permissive  climate and environment for aid workers  so i think engagement will be uh  important in order to set early tests  and what we'll be judging the taliban by  is their actions not their words  so from the french news agency  afp  i have to question sheikh mohammed  brahman  qatari technical team already arrived in  kabul airport is qatar planning to  operate the airport  as the head of the afghan aviation  authority already said that they are  ready to sign partnership agreement with  qatar  for  minister dominik  what might encourage the uk to  make relations with  the taliban  thank you  well uh  regarding regarding the airport  actually  right now we are in an assessment and  surveying  period if qatar help is going to make a  difference and make the airport  up or running sooner definitely you will  not hesitate to help and support that  process but  there is no agreement yet how this form  of operations going uh to take place and  still  uh under the discussion as i just  mentioned we are trying our best to  support now  uh bringing back  the airport to be up and running and  this is this is remaining our focus in  the next short term period  thank you the  reality is as i as i described uh we  will uh not be recognizing the taliban  any time uh in the foreseeable future  but i think there is an important scope  for  engagement and dialogue  and to test the intentions and indeed  the assurances that have already been  made by the taliban safe passage out of  afghanistan for um and i'm afraid to my  friend from the bbc i didn't mention it  but to those afghan workers or  contractors  who are eligible and still want to come  to the uk or indeed other countries  i think the commitments they've made not  to allow afghanistan to be  a base or a safe haven for terrorist  groups the permissive environment for  humanitarian  workers they've talked about  forming a more inclusive government in  all of these areas we will judge them  but what they by what they do not just  by what they say

